5_step_2.md\nprototype_1_5_step_3.md\nprototype_1_5_step_4.md\nprototype_1_5_step_5.md\nprototype_1_5_step_6.md\nprototype_1.md\nprototype_1_step_1.md\nprototype_1_step_2.md\nprototype_1_step_3.md\nprototype_1_step_4.md\nprototype_1_step_5.md\nquality-practices.md\nquestions.md\nselect-materialization-research.md\nsignature-matching-deeper-research.md\nstdout-stderr-effect-model-research.md\nstreams.md\nstring-type-spike.md\ntrait-interface-research.md\ntype-flow.md\nworker-pool.md\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls plans/","time":{"start":1788686235310,"end":1788686235312}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The issue is \"benchmark-fasta-build\" - this is a lane name, likely not a real GitHub issue. The `gh issue view` expects a number. Let me try looking at the plans directory, benchmark-plan.md.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07601f62c001ohipHgZhGCAOjS","sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","messageID":"msg_07601efe1001Lff7KOkVD1fFEo"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788686236602,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a09a422da01a05c9","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/ESCALATION.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/ESCALATION.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Escalation: `just check` cannot go green as root\n2: \n3: ## Question\n4: \n5: The `search-layer-build` brief requires `just check` green. Two pre-existing tests,\n6: `tests/fmt_project.rs::unreadable_directory_is_reported_and_walk_continues` and\n7: `::unreadable_file_is_reported_and_walk_continues`, cannot pass when the suite runs as root:\n8: they chmod a directory or file to `0o000` and assert that the `toylang fmt` walker reports a\n9: permission error. Root bypasses permission bits, so `read_dir`/`read` succeed and the refusal\n10: never happens.\n11: \n12: ## Evidence it is environmental, not caused by this branch\n13: \n14: - The failing assertion is exactly `read_dir` on a `0o000` directory. Verified in this sandbox:\n15:   as root, `ls` reads the directory; as `nobody` (via `setpriv`), it gets `Permission denied`.\n16: - Running the already-built `fmt_project` test binary as `nobody` passes both tests unchanged.\n17: - CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on `ubuntu-latest`, a non-root user, where both pass.\n18: \n19: None of this branch's changes (the `first`/`any`/`all` builtins) touch file permissions or the\n20: formatter.\n21: \n22: ## Alternatives\n23: \n24: 1. **Skip the two tests when running as root.** Minimal, transparent, keeps the assertions\n25:    fully active for the non-root case that CI and normal development run. The chosen path.\n26: 2. **Run the whole suite as a non-root user in this sandbox.** Requires giving a non-root user\n27:    write access to `/repo` (the suite rewrites `tests/corpus/*.yaml` and\n28:    `site/public/corpus.json` in place) and a copy of `CARGO_HOME`; invasive and fragile.\n29: 3. **Leave `just check` red and document only.** Fails the brief's explicit definition of done.\n30: \n31: ## Decision\n32: \n33: Took alternative 1: a `running_as_root()` guard (`id -u` reports 0) at the top of the two\n34: tests, with a comment explaining why the assertion is unobservable as root. The guard does not\n35: silently cover a broken walker: for any non-root user the tests run exactly as before. Revert\n36: this if the maintainer prefers a different accommodation.\n\n(End of file - total 36 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Escalation: `just check` cannot go green as root\n\n## Question\n\nThe `search-layer-build` brief requires `just check` green. Two pre-existing tests,\n`tests/fmt_project.rs::unreadable_directory_is_reported_and_walk_continues` and\n`::unreadable_file_is_reported_and_walk_continues`, cannot pass when the suite runs as root:\nthey chmod a directory or file to `0o000` and assert that the `toylang fmt` walker reports a\npermission error. Root bypasses permission bits, so `read_dir`/`read` succeed and the refusal\nnever happens.\n\n## Evidence it is environmental, not caused by this branch\n\n- The failing assertion is exactly `read_dir` on a `0o000` directory. Verified in this sandbox:\n  as root, `ls` reads the directory; as `nobody` (via `setpriv`), it gets `Permission denied`.\n- Running the already-built `fmt_project` test binary as `nobody` passes both tests unchanged.\n- CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on `ubuntu-latest`, a non-root user, where both pass.\n\nNone of this branch's changes (the `first`/`any`/`all` builtins) touch file permissions or the\nformatter.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/ESCALATION.md","text":"# Escalation: `just check` cannot go green as root\n\n## Question\n\nThe `search-layer-build` brief requires `just check` green. Two pre-existing tests,\n`tests/fmt_project.rs::unreadable_directory_is_reported_and_walk_continues` and\n`::unreadable_file_is_reported_and_walk_continues`, cannot pass when the suite runs as root:\nthey chmod a directory or file to `0o000` and assert that the `toylang fmt` walker reports a\npermission error. Root bypasses permission bits, so `read_dir`/`read` succeed and the refusal\nnever happens.\n\n## Evidence it is environmental, not caused by this branch\n\n- The failing assertion is exactly `read_dir` on a `0o000` directory. Verified in this sandbox:\n  as root, `ls` reads the directory; as `nobody` (via `setpriv`), it gets `Permission denied`.\n- Running the already-built `fmt_project` test binary as `nobody` passes both tests unchanged.\n- CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on `ubuntu-latest`, a non-root user, where both pass.\n\nNone of this branch's changes (the `first`/`any`/`all` builtins) touch file permissions or the\nformatter.\n\n## Alternatives\n\n1. **Skip the two tests when running as root.** Minimal, transparent, keeps the assertions\n   fully active for the non-root case that CI and normal development run. The chosen path.\n2. **Run the whole suite as a non-root user in this sandbox.** Requires giving a non-root user\n   write access to `/repo` (the suite rewrites `tests/corpus/*.yaml` and\n   `site/public/corpus.json` in place) and a copy of `CARGO_HOME`; invasive and fragile.\n3. **Leave `just check` red and document only.** Fails the brief's explicit definition of done.\n\n## Decision\n\nTook alternative 1: a `running_as_root()` guard (`id -u` reports 0) at the top of the two\ntests, with a comment explaining why the assertion is unobservable as root. The guard does not\nsilently cover a broken walker: for any non-root user the tests run exactly as before. Revert\nthis if the maintainer prefers a different accommodation.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":36,"totalLines":36,"truncated":false}},"title":"ESCALATION.md","time":{"start":1788686236596,"end":1788686236601}},"id":"prt_07601f6ab0015fOg5xh2u8FoxX","sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","messageID":"msg_07601efe1001Lff7KOkVD1fFEo"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788686236912,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9f344127048d4938","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/CONTEXT.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/CONTEXT.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # toylang\n2: \n3: A compiled, statically typed language for transforming data, taking jq as its main inspiration\n4: without aiming at compatibility with it. This file is the glossary and nothing else: what the\n5: terms mean, not how anything is built. The design lives in `draft.md`, the build order in\n6: `plans/`, and what building it taught us in `research-log/`.\n7: \n8: ## Language\n9: \n10: ### Shape of a value\n11: \n12: **Scalar**:\n13: A value with no interior to address: `Int`, `Str`, `Bool`.\n14: \n15: **Record**:\n16: A fixed set of differently-typed parts addressed by name, where the names are part of the type.\n17: Type theory calls this a product, which is exact and says nothing to a reader who has not met the\n18: word, so that name is not used here.\n19: _Avoid_: object, struct, row, product\n20: \n21: **Dimension**:\n22: An axis along which a value repeats, addressed by position, whose entries all have the same type.\n23: A `Vec` has one; a tensor has several. A type's dimensions are fixed by the type, in order.\n24: _Avoid_: axis, rank, column\n25: \n26: **Extent**:\n27: How many entries a dimension has. A number, not a type.\n28: _Avoid_: size, cardinality\n29: \n30: **Field**:\n31: One named part of a record. `name` is a field of `{name: Str, age: Int}`.\n32: _Avoid_: column, attribute, component\n33: \n34: **Map**:\n35: A collection whose keys are data rather than type-level, with one value type. Distinct from a\n36: record: a record's keys are known to the compiler, a map's are known only to the program.\n37: _Avoid_: object, dict, record\n38: \n39: **Name casing**:\n40: A name beginning with a capital letter is a type; a name beginning with a lowercase letter is a\n41: value, which covers functions and their parameters. Field names are exempt, because they come\n42: from data and a JSON object is entitled to a key spelled `Name`.\n43: \n44: ### Building a value\n45: \n46: **Record literal**:\n47: The form that builds a record from its fields, and the inverse of a projection.\n48: `{name: .n, age: .a}` is one. Its type is the names and types of its fields, so it answers what\n49: it is from its contents alone and never needs its position to say. In argument position it is\n50: also how a function takes more than one thing, because a function takes one argument and a record\n51: is how several travel as one.\n52: _Avoid_: object literal, struct literal, product literal, construction\n53: \n54: **Vec literal**:\n55: The form that builds a dimension from its entries. `[1, 2]` is one. Unlike a record literal it\n56: cannot always answer what it is, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\n57: empty one has none.\n58: _Avoid_: array literal, list literal\n59: \n60: **Application**:\n61: Calling a function with its one argument. Two spellings, one meaning: `f x`, the bare form, is\n62: the default, and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped -- the disambiguator when the\n63: bare form would read differently (an argument starting with `-`, `.`, or `[`, a compound\n64: argument, or a call spread across lines, since an argument must start on the same line as its\n65: function). Both work anywhere an atom does, including as an operand. `select` and `map` are not\n66: special syntax -- they are ordinary names reached through this same rule.\n67: _Avoid_: call, invocation, function call\n68: \n69: ### Reaching into a value\n70: \n71: **Spec**:\n72: What an access says about one dimension: keep it, narrow it, or collapse it. Every dimension of a\n73: value needs one, which is why `[]` is written rather than assumed.\n74: \n75: **Keep**:\n76: A spec that leaves a dimension at full extent, written `[]`. Streamable, since it consumes\n77: nothing.\n78: \n79: **Narrow**:\n80: A spec that leaves a dimension at reduced extent, such as a mask. Streamable.\n81: \n82: **Collapse**:\n83: A spec that removes a dimension, such as an index. Not streamable: it has to consume to find the\n84: entry, so on a stream it destroys what it passed.\n85: \n86: **Selection**:\n87: Choosing along a dimension -- which entries. `select` and index specs are selections.\n88: _Avoid_: filter, projection\n89: \n90: **Projection**:\n91: Choosing a field out of a record -- which part of each entry. `.name` is a projection.\n92: _Avoid_: select, field access, column selection\n93: \n94: ### Guarantees\n95: \n96: **Rectangular**:\n97: Every entry of a dimension has the same extent in the next dimension down. A refinement, not a\n98: requirement: it is what makes collapsing an inner dimension total, so a non-rectangular value\n99: yields `Opt` where entries are missing rather than being inaccessible.\n100: _Avoid_: dense, uniform, matrix-shaped\n101: \n102: **Ragged**:\n103: Not rectangular. Dimensions are still enumerable and still addressable; only totality is lost.\n104: _Avoid_: jagged, irregular\n105: \n106: **Enumerable dimensions**:\n107: A type fixes how deep it goes, so its dimensions can be named in order. `Vec<Vec<Int>>` has two.\n108: A type of unbounded depth has none, and is reached only by recursive descent.\n109: \n110: ### Choosing among shapes\n111: \n112: **Enum**:\n113: A declared, closed set of named variants, nominal: the name is the identity, and consuming one\n114: must handle every variant. As data it is plain JSON, never an opaque value.\n115: _Avoid_: union, sum type, ADT, tagged union\n116: \n117: **Variant**:\n118: One named alternative of an enum, optionally carrying a payload of one type -- and itself a\n119: type, a subtype of its enum, so a signature can name it. Capitalized, per the casing rule,\n120: because it is a type. As data: the single-key wrapper, `{\"Circle\": {\"r\": 1}}`, the name\n121: verbatim.\n122: _Avoid_: case, constructor, alternative\n123: \n124: **Unit variant**:\n125: A variant with no payload. As data: a bare string, the name verbatim (`\"Active\"`). Wild\n126: lowercase string enums are no longer directly typeable; they wait for the codec layer.\n127: _Avoid_: nullary constructor, bare tag\n128: \n129: ### Where multiplicity lives\n130: \n131: **Stream**:\n132: The type of effect-layer multiplicity: `Stream<T>` says an expression yields its entries one at\n133: a time as evaluation proceeds, not that a stream object exists. Born at a source, consumed\n134: exactly once, dead at `collect` or a sink; never inside a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`.\n135: _Avoid_: lazy list, generator, iterator, channel\n136: \n137: **Source**:\n138: An expression a `Stream` is born from, and the only way one arises: `inputs`, `lines`.\n139: _Avoid_: producer, reader\n140: \n141: **Sink**:\n142: A stream consumer legal only as a program's outermost expression, writing as it goes, with no\n143: result type. `jsonlines` is one.\n144: _Avoid_: printer, writer, output function\n145: \n146: **Collect**:\n147: The named spelling of reify at the stream boundary: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`, the one explicit way\n148: a `Stream` becomes a value. Not a third layer shifter.\n149: _Avoid_: materialize, realize, to_vec\n\n(End of file - total 149 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# toylang\n\nA compiled, statically typed language for transforming data, taking jq as its main inspiration\nwithout aiming at compatibility with it. This file is the glossary and nothing else: what the\nterms mean, not how anything is built. The design lives in `draft.md`, the build order in\n`plans/`, and what building it taught us in `research-log/`.\n\n## Language\n\n### Shape of a value\n\n**Scalar**:\nA value with no interior to address: `Int`, `Str`, `Bool`.\n\n**Record**:\nA fixed set of differently-typed parts addressed by name, where the names are part of the type.\nType theory calls this a product, which is exact and says nothing to a reader who has not met the\nword, so that name is not used here.\n_Avoid_: object, struct, row, product\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/CONTEXT.md","text":"# toylang\n\nA compiled, statically typed language for transforming data, taking jq as its main inspiration\nwithout aiming at compatibility with it. This file is the glossary and nothing else: what the\nterms mean, not how anything is built. The design lives in `draft.md`, the build order in\n`plans/`, and what building it taught us in `research-log/`.\n\n## Language\n\n### Shape of a value\n\n**Scalar**:\nA value with no interior to address: `Int`, `Str`, `Bool`.\n\n**Record**:\nA fixed set of differently-typed parts addressed by name, where the names are part of the type.\nType theory calls this a product, which is exact and says nothing to a reader who has not met the\nword, so that name is not used here.\n_Avoid_: object, struct, row, product\n\n**Dimension**:\nAn axis along which a value repeats, addressed by position, whose entries all have the same type.\nA `Vec` has one; a tensor has several. A type's dimensions are fixed by the type, in order.\n_Avoid_: axis, rank, column\n\n**Extent**:\nHow many entries a dimension has. A number, not a type.\n_Avoid_: size, cardinality\n\n**Field**:\nOne named part of a record. `name` is a field of `{name: Str, age: Int}`.\n_Avoid_: column, attribute, component\n\n**Map**:\nA collection whose keys are data rather than type-level, with one value type. Distinct from a\nrecord: a record's keys are known to the compiler, a map's are known only to the program.\n_Avoid_: object, dict, record\n\n**Name casing**:\nA name beginning with a capital letter is a type; a name beginning with a lowercase letter is a\nvalue, which covers functions and their parameters. Field names are exempt, because they come\nfrom data and a JSON object is entitled to a key spelled `Name`.\n\n### Building a value\n\n**Record literal**:\nThe form that builds a record from its fields, and the inverse of a projection.\n`{name: .n, age: .a}` is one. Its type is the names and types of its fields, so it answers what\nit is from its contents alone and never needs its position to say. In argument position it is\nalso how a function takes more than one thing, because a function takes one argument and a record\nis how several travel as one.\n_Avoid_: object literal, struct literal, product literal, construction\n\n**Vec literal**:\nThe form that builds a dimension from its entries. `[1, 2]` is one. Unlike a record literal it\ncannot always answer what it is, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\nempty one has none.\n_Avoid_: array literal, list literal\n\n**Application**:\nCalling a function with its one argument. Two spellings, one meaning: `f x`, the bare form, is\nthe default, and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped -- the disambiguator when the\nbare form would read differently (an argument starting with `-`, `.`, or `[`, a compound\nargument, or a call spread across lines, since an argument must start on the same line as its\nfunction). Both work anywhere an atom does, including as an operand. `select` and `map` are not\nspecial syntax -- they are ordinary names reached through this same rule.\n_Avoid_: call, invocation, function call\n\n### Reaching into a value\n\n**Spec**:\nWhat an access says about one dimension: keep it, narrow it, or collapse it. Every dimension of a\nvalue needs one, which is why `[]` is written rather than assumed.\n\n**Keep**:\nA spec that leaves a dimension at full extent, written `[]`. Streamable, since it consumes\nnothing.\n\n**Narrow**:\nA spec that leaves a dimension at reduced extent, such as a mask. Streamable.\n\n**Collapse**:\nA spec that removes a dimension, such as an index. Not streamable: it has to consume to find the\nentry, so on a stream it destroys what it passed.\n\n**Selection**:\nChoosing along a dimension -- which entries. `select` and index specs are selections.\n_Avoid_: filter, projection\n\n**Projection**:\nChoosing a field out of a record -- which part of each entry. `.name` is a projection.\n_Avoid_: select, field access, column selection\n\n### Guarantees\n\n**Rectangular**:\nEvery entry of a dimension has the same extent in the next dimension down. A refinement, not a\nrequirement: it is what makes collapsing an inner dimension total, so a non-rectangular value\nyields `Opt` where entries are missing rather than being inaccessible.\n_Avoid_: dense, uniform, matrix-shaped\n\n**Ragged**:\nNot rectangular. Dimensions are still enumerable and still addressable; only totality is lost.\n_Avoid_: jagged, irregular\n\n**Enumerable dimensions**:\nA type fixes how deep it goes, so its dimensions can be named in order. `Vec<Vec<Int>>` has two.\nA type of unbounded depth has none, and is reached only by recursive descent.\n\n### Choosing among shapes\n\n**Enum**:\nA declared, closed set of named variants, nominal: the name is the identity, and consuming one\nmust handle every variant. As data it is plain JSON, never an opaque value.\n_Avoid_: union, sum type, ADT, tagged union\n\n**Variant**:\nOne named alternative of an enum, optionally carrying a payload of one type -- and itself a\ntype, a subtype of its enum, so a signature can name it. Capitalized, per the casing rule,\nbecause it is a type. As data: the single-key wrapper, `{\"Circle\": {\"r\": 1}}`, the name\nverbatim.\n_Avoid_: case, constructor, alternative\n\n**Unit variant**:\nA variant with no payload. As data: a bare string, the name verbatim (`\"Active\"`). Wild\nlowercase string enums are no longer directly typeable; they wait for the codec layer.\n_Avoid_: nullary constructor, bare tag\n\n### Where multiplicity lives\n\n**Stream**:\nThe type of effect-layer multiplicity: `Stream<T>` says an expression yields its entries one at\na time as evaluation proceeds, not that a stream object exists. Born at a source, consumed\nexactly once, dead at `collect` or a sink; never inside a record, a `Vec`, or another `Stream`.\n_Avoid_: lazy list, generator, iterator, channel\n\n**Source**:\nAn expression a `Stream` is born from, and the only way one arises: `inputs`, `lines`.\n_Avoid_: producer, reader\n\n**Sink**:\nA stream consumer legal only as a program's outermost expression, writing as it goes, with no\nresult type. `jsonlines` is one.\n_Avoid_: printer, writer, output function\n\n**Collect**:\nThe named spelling of reify at the stream boundary: `Stream<T> -> Vec<T>`, the one explicit way\na `Stream` becomes a value. Not a third layer shifter.\n_Avoid_: materialize, realize, to_vec","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":149,"totalLines":149,"truncated":false}},"title":"CONTEXT.md","time":{"start":1788686236901,"end":1788686236910}},"id":"prt_07601fbb6001WStqHfogZfTNZg","sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","messageID":"msg_07601efe1001Lff7KOkVD1fFEo"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788686236926,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","part":{"id":"prt_07601fcf9001CKCTnnEbSZTjPL","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"e455b591a29bff5c36ed2ab3c7c0219ec29ad191","messageID":"msg_07601efe1001Lff7KOkVD1fFEo","sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":19928,"input":270,"output":154,"reasoning":48,"cache":{"write":0,"read":19456}},"cost":0.000228168696}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788686238342,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","part":{"id":"prt_07602027d001oirlul1ub9OL43","messageID":"msg_07601fd0d001M277qyoJoh2tsZ","sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","snapshot":"e455b591a29bff5c36ed2ab3c7c0219ec29ad191","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788686241676,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-aef32430b29a2be7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/plans/benchmark-plan.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/plans/benchmark-plan.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Benchmark plan: the set, the harness, and how the numbers compare\n2: \n3: Synthesis of the two spikes: the program set from gh:106\n4: ([`benchmark-suite-spike.md`](benchmark-suite-spike.md)) and the harness from gh:107\n5: ([`benchmark-tooling-spike.md`](benchmark-tooling-spike.md)). Each spike already settled its half;\n6: this file is where the two halves stop being separate questions and become one runnable shape.\n7: Where the spikes left a decision open, this plan says what it assumes and names what it does not.\n8: \n9: ## The program set\n10: \n11: Adopt the ten task names from the Computer Language Benchmarks Game, license-clean under\n12: BSD-3-Clause, written from each task's plain description in toylang and cited as derived from the\n13: CLBG set (`Derived: Computer Language Benchmarks Game task set, BSD-3-Clause,\n14: https://benchmarksgame-team.pages.debian.net/benchmarksgame/`). Write the programs the way this\n15: repo already treats Project Euler: paraphrase the problem, never copy a reference implementation.\n16: \n17: Feasibility today splits by toylang's own shape, not by CLBG's categories:\n18: \n19: - **Good fit:** fasta, k-nucleotide, reverse-complement, regex-redux -- text and stream\n20:   processing, the language's design center.\n21: - **Reachable with the recursion work already landed:** fannkuch-redux, binary-trees, pidigits.\n22: - **Open:** n-body and spectral-norm, tight floating-point loops over mutable accumulators. See\n23:   the escalation note below; the plan's default is to keep them named in the set but not blocked\n24:   on them.\n25: \n26: That split is the one real judgment call the set spike leaves to this plan, and it is not settled\n27: by the spikes' text, so it is flagged rather than silently decided.\n28: \n29: ## The harness\n30: \n31: hyperfine drives every backend as a real subprocess, using `--parameter-list` to sweep one\n32: command template across the seven backends, `--warmup` to stabilize Go and the JIT-adjacent\n33: interpreters, `--input <file>` to feed corpus fixtures where a case has one, and\n34: `--export-markdown`/`--export-json` for reporting. The arguments for this over criterion and\n35: divan, and why the in-process tools are the right fit only for a future compiler-throughput\n36: benchmark, are in the tooling spike and are not restated here.\n37: \n38: The benchmark runner is a thin sibling of `run_on` (`src/lib.rs`), not `run_on` itself. Three\n39: retargetings make the timed number mean the same thing per backend:\n40: \n41: - **Go:** `run_go` uses `go run`, which compiles on every sample. Build once with `go build`\n42:   outside the timed loop, then let hyperfine time the binary the way it times Rust and Native.\n43: - **Lua:** `run_lua` runs inside the process through the embedded `mlua::Lua`. hyperfine can only\n44:   time a spawned process, so write the emitted source to a file and run it through the system\n45:   `lua5.4` (already a CI dependency) -- which also makes Lua's number comparable, real spawn plus\n46:   real interpreter startup like every other row.\n47: - **Rust and Native** already compile ahead of the run via `link_rust` and `link` and hand back a\n48:   bare executable path; reuse those two functions directly.\n49: \n50: So the runner shares the emit step with `run_on` but splits compile-once from run-many, which is\n51: exactly the boundary `run_on` currently blurs.\n52: \n53: ## How results compare against other languages\n54: \n55: A comparison needs the same program in toylang and in the comparison languages, run by the same\n56: harness. The concrete shape:\n57: \n58: - toylang runs across its seven backends from the emitted source, as above.\n59: - The comparison rows are the same task written in jq, Python, Node, Go, Rust, C, and Lua --\n60:   toylang's own backend languages are the obvious baseline set, because they are already\n61:   installed and the comparison asks \"what does this emitted program cost next to the language it\n62:   is emitted into?\" rather than \"how does toylang compare to an arbitrary third party.\"\n63: - Every row is timed by the same hyperfine invocation with the same `--warmup`, `--runs`, and\n64:   input, so the only variable is the language/backend the program was written or emitted in.\n65: \n66: Carry the CLBG site's own caveat wherever the numbers land: the game's maintainers call its\n67: results \"far from realistic\" and not a general performance ranking. These timings are comparative\n68: color across this project's backends and their host languages, not a claim about toylang in the\n69: abstract.\n70: \n71: ## CI reporting\n72: \n73: Post hyperfine's `--export-markdown` table as a job summary or PR comment -- enough to answer\n74: \"did this change make anything slower\" by eye on a single run. The regression-dashboard option\n75: (`github-action-benchmark`, requiring a small JSON conversion from `--export-json`) stays a real\n76: option, deliberately not built now: there is no baseline worth protecting until this plan runs\n77: for the first time, and a moving dashboard is only worth its upkeep once a specific regression it\n78: would have caught actually happens.\n79: \n80: ## Build status (2026-09-03)\n81: \n82: The harness landed as designed: `src/bin/bench.rs` (`just bench NAME`) compiles a\n83: `benches/programs/<name>.toy` benchmark once, builds Go/Rust/Native ahead of the timed run,\n84: retargets Lua at the system `lua5.4` (writing the `t_input` global into the script text itself,\n85: since there is no embedding host to set it the way `run_lua` does), and drives `hyperfine`\n86: `--shell=none` over all seven. Results export to `benches/results/<name>.{md,json}` (gitignored).\n87: Today's harness feeds at most one `Int` value from stdin, or nothing -- see the doc comment atop\n88: `bench.rs` for what extending it to a richer input type needs.\n89: \n90: `binary-trees` is the one task landed so far (`benches/programs/binary-trees.toy`, correctness\n91: pinned at `tests/corpus/binary_trees_node_count.yaml`): build a perfect binary tree of a given\n92: depth, count its nodes. Simplified from CLBG's actual multi-tree, GC-stress variant (a stretch\n93: tree, a long-lived tree, and a loop of many trees at each depth) to the single build-and-count\n94: core, which is what the language can express today; the loop-of-many-trees shape adds nothing a\n95: recursive count doesn't already exercise, so it was left out rather than force-fit.\n96: \n97: **The suite spike's \"good fit today\" claim for fasta/k-nucleotide/reverse-complement/regex-redux\n98: does not hold against the current builtin set.** `chars(s)` decodes `Str` to `Vec<Char>`, but\n99: `Char` has no wire form and there is no `Char -> Str` builtin and no `Str` slice/index operator\n100: (`docs/reference/types/char.md`; confirmed empirically, not just read off the docs, since the\n101: docs could themselves be stale) -- so a program cannot decode an existing string, transform it\n102: character by character, and print the result. That blocks reverse-complement (decode, complement,\n103: re-encode) and k-nucleotide (decode into k-length windows, print each as a string) outright.\n104: regex-redux additionally has no regex engine to build on. fasta is different: it only *generates*\n105: text from a small fixed alphabet of literal `Str` values chosen by a computed index, never\n106: decodes anything, so it stays buildable -- just not yet built. pidigits needs digits of pi beyond\n107: what a 32-bit or even 64-bit accumulator holds; the spigot algorithm's usual unbounded-bignum\n108: shape has no home in a language with no bignum type, so it is blocked the same way the float\n109: tasks are, on a type the language does not have yet.\n110: \n111: Of the eight, `binary-trees` is landed; `fasta` and `fannkuch-redux` (both fully numeric or\n112: literal-driven, no decode needed) are the next real candidates; `mandelbrot` needs float support\n113: most backends don't have yet, the same gate n-body/spectral-norm are already behind; `pidigits`\n114: needs a bignum type; `reverse-complement`, `k-nucleotide`, and `regex-redux` need `Str`\n115: slicing or a `Char -> Str` builtin, neither of which exists.\n116: \n117: ## What this plan leaves to the next person\n118: \n119: - Whether n-body and spectral-norm stay in the initial set, and whether float semantics (the\n120:   `q37-float-semantics` / `float-build` board rows, both still `todo`) gate them.\n121: - A dashboard, when there is a baseline and a specific regression it would have caught.\n122: \n123: These are named rather than resolved here because the spikes do not settle them.\n\n(End of file - total 123 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Benchmark plan: the set, the harness, and how the numbers compare\n\nSynthesis of the two spikes: the program set from gh:106\n([`benchmark-suite-spike.md`](benchmark-suite-spike.md)) and the harness from gh:107\n([`benchmark-tooling-spike.md`](benchmark-tooling-spike.md)). Each spike already settled its half;\nthis file is where the two halves stop being separate questions and become one runnable shape.\nWhere the spikes left a decision open, this plan says what it assumes and names what it does not.\n\n## The program set\n\nAdopt the ten task names from the Computer Language Benchmarks Game, license-clean under\nBSD-3-Clause, written from each task's plain description in toylang and cited as derived from the\nCLBG set (`Derived: Computer Language Benchmarks Game task set, BSD-3-Clause,\nhttps://benchmarksgame-team.pages.debian.net/benchmarksgame/`). Write the programs the way this\nrepo already treats Project Euler: paraphrase the problem, never copy a reference implementation.\n\nFeasibility today splits by toylang's own shape, not by CLBG's categories:\n\n- **Good fit:** fasta, k-nucleotide, reverse-complement, regex-redux -- text and stream\n  processing, the language's design center.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/plans/benchmark-plan.md","text":"# Benchmark plan: the set, the harness, and how the numbers compare\n\nSynthesis of the two spikes: the program set from gh:106\n([`benchmark-suite-spike.md`](benchmark-suite-spike.md)) and the harness from gh:107\n([`benchmark-tooling-spike.md`](benchmark-tooling-spike.md)). Each spike already settled its half;\nthis file is where the two halves stop being separate questions and become one runnable shape.\nWhere the spikes left a decision open, this plan says what it assumes and names what it does not.\n\n## The program set\n\nAdopt the ten task names from the Computer Language Benchmarks Game, license-clean under\nBSD-3-Clause, written from each task's plain description in toylang and cited as derived from the\nCLBG set (`Derived: Computer Language Benchmarks Game task set, BSD-3-Clause,\nhttps://benchmarksgame-team.pages.debian.net/benchmarksgame/`). Write the programs the way this\nrepo already treats Project Euler: paraphrase the problem, never copy a reference implementation.\n\nFeasibility today splits by toylang's own shape, not by CLBG's categories:\n\n- **Good fit:** fasta, k-nucleotide, reverse-complement, regex-redux -- text and stream\n  processing, the language's design center.\n- **Reachable with the recursion work already landed:** fannkuch-redux, binary-trees, pidigits.\n- **Open:** n-body and spectral-norm, tight floating-point loops over mutable accumulators. See\n  the escalation note below; the plan's default is to keep them named in the set but not blocked\n  on them.\n\nThat split is the one real judgment call the set spike leaves to this plan, and it is not settled\nby the spikes' text, so it is flagged rather than silently decided.\n\n## The harness\n\nhyperfine drives every backend as a real subprocess, using `--parameter-list` to sweep one\ncommand template across the seven backends, `--warmup` to stabilize Go and the JIT-adjacent\ninterpreters, `--input <file>` to feed corpus fixtures where a case has one, and\n`--export-markdown`/`--export-json` for reporting. The arguments for this over criterion and\ndivan, and why the in-process tools are the right fit only for a future compiler-throughput\nbenchmark, are in the tooling spike and are not restated here.\n\nThe benchmark runner is a thin sibling of `run_on` (`src/lib.rs`), not `run_on` itself. Three\nretargetings make the timed number mean the same thing per backend:\n\n- **Go:** `run_go` uses `go run`, which compiles on every sample. Build once with `go build`\n  outside the timed loop, then let hyperfine time the binary the way it times Rust and Native.\n- **Lua:** `run_lua` runs inside the process through the embedded `mlua::Lua`. hyperfine can only\n  time a spawned process, so write the emitted source to a file and run it through the system\n  `lua5.4` (already a CI dependency) -- which also makes Lua's number comparable, real spawn plus\n  real interpreter startup like every other row.\n- **Rust and Native** already compile ahead of the run via `link_rust` and `link` and hand back a\n  bare executable path; reuse those two functions directly.\n\nSo the runner shares the emit step with `run_on` but splits compile-once from run-many, which is\nexactly the boundary `run_on` currently blurs.\n\n## How results compare against other languages\n\nA comparison needs the same program in toylang and in the comparison languages, run by the same\nharness. The concrete shape:\n\n- toylang runs across its seven backends from the emitted source, as above.\n- The comparison rows are the same task written in jq, Python, Node, Go, Rust, C, and Lua --\n  toylang's own backend languages are the obvious baseline set, because they are already\n  installed and the comparison asks \"what does this emitted program cost next to the language it\n  is emitted into?\" rather than \"how does toylang compare to an arbitrary third party.\"\n- Every row is timed by the same hyperfine invocation with the same `--warmup`, `--runs`, and\n  input, so the only variable is the language/backend the program was written or emitted in.\n\nCarry the CLBG site's own caveat wherever the numbers land: the game's maintainers call its\nresults \"far from realistic\" and not a general performance ranking. These timings are comparative\ncolor across this project's backends and their host languages, not a claim about toylang in the\nabstract.\n\n## CI reporting\n\nPost hyperfine's `--export-markdown` table as a job summary or PR comment -- enough to answer\n\"did this change make anything slower\" by eye on a single run. The regression-dashboard option\n(`github-action-benchmark`, requiring a small JSON conversion from `--export-json`) stays a real\noption, deliberately not built now: there is no baseline worth protecting until this plan runs\nfor the first time, and a moving dashboard is only worth its upkeep once a specific regression it\nwould have caught actually happens.\n\n## Build status (2026-09-03)\n\nThe harness landed as designed: `src/bin/bench.rs` (`just bench NAME`) compiles a\n`benches/programs/<name>.toy` benchmark once, builds Go/Rust/Native ahead of the timed run,\nretargets Lua at the system `lua5.4` (writing the `t_input` global into the script text itself,\nsince there is no embedding host to set it the way `run_lua` does), and drives `hyperfine`\n`--shell=none` over all seven. Results export to `benches/results/<name>.{md,json}` (gitignored).\nToday's harness feeds at most one `Int` value from stdin, or nothing -- see the doc comment atop\n`bench.rs` for what extending it to a richer input type needs.\n\n`binary-trees` is the one task landed so far (`benches/programs/binary-trees.toy`, correctness\npinned at `tests/corpus/binary_trees_node_count.yaml`): build a perfect binary tree of a given\ndepth, count its nodes. Simplified from CLBG's actual multi-tree, GC-stress variant (a stretch\ntree, a long-lived tree, and a loop of many trees at each depth) to the single build-and-count\ncore, which is what the language can express today; the loop-of-many-trees shape adds nothing a\nrecursive count doesn't already exercise, so it was left out rather than force-fit.\n\n**The suite spike's \"good fit today\" claim for fasta/k-nucleotide/reverse-complement/regex-redux\ndoes not hold against the current builtin set.** `chars(s)` decodes `Str` to `Vec<Char>`, but\n`Char` has no wire form and there is no `Char -> Str` builtin and no `Str` slice/index operator\n(`docs/reference/types/char.md`; confirmed empirically, not just read off the docs, since the\ndocs could themselves be stale) -- so a program cannot decode an existing string, transform it\ncharacter by character, and print the result. That blocks reverse-complement (decode, complement,\nre-encode) and k-nucleotide (decode into k-length windows, print each as a string) outright.\nregex-redux additionally has no regex engine to build on. fasta is different: it only *generates*\ntext from a small fixed alphabet of literal `Str` values chosen by a computed index, never\ndecodes anything, so it stays buildable -- just not yet built. pidigits needs digits of pi beyond\nwhat a 32-bit or even 64-bit accumulator holds; the spigot algorithm's usual unbounded-bignum\nshape has no home in a language with no bignum type, so it is blocked the same way the float\ntasks are, on a type the language does not have yet.\n\nOf the eight, `binary-trees` is landed; `fasta` and `fannkuch-redux` (both fully numeric or\nliteral-driven, no decode needed) are the next real candidates; `mandelbrot` needs float support\nmost backends don't have yet, the same gate n-body/spectral-norm are already behind; `pidigits`\nneeds a bignum type; `reverse-complement`, `k-nucleotide`, and `regex-redux` need `Str`\nslicing or a `Char -> Str` builtin, neither of which exists.\n\n## What this plan leaves to the next person\n\n- Whether n-body and spectral-norm stay in the initial set, and whether float semantics (the\n  `q37-float-semantics` / `float-build` board rows, both still `todo`) gate them.\n- A dashboard, when there is a baseline and a specific regression it would have caught.\n\nThese are named rather than resolved here because the spikes do not settle them.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":123,"totalLines":123,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/benchmark-plan.md","time":{"start":1788686241632,"end":1788686241637}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The issue \"benchmark-fasta-build\" is not a GitHub issue number. Let me look at the board/plan docs. Let me read the benchmark-plan.md and board.yaml to understand the board row. The brief says \"Board row benchmark-fasta-build (benchmark-plan.md Build status)\". Let me look at the plan.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_076020b23001j4YKr4zKG6egJr","sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","messageID":"msg_07601fd0d001M277qyoJoh2tsZ"}}
