{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788686293010,"sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b8a6f922855a799f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/benches/programs/binary-trees.toy"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/benches/programs/binary-trees.toy</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: enum Tree { Leaf, Node(Vec<Tree>) }\n2: \n3: fn build(depth: Int) -> Tree =\n4:     depth | . == 0 -> Tree.leaf or Tree.node([build(. - 1), build(. - 1)])\n5: \n6: fn count(t: Tree) -> Int =\n7:     t | Leaf -> 1 or Node -> 1 + count(.[0]!) + count(.[1]!)\n8: \n9: count(build(parse(stdin)))\n\n(End of file - total 9 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"enum Tree { Leaf, Node(Vec<Tree>) }\n\nfn build(depth: Int) -> Tree =\n    depth | . == 0 -> Tree.leaf or Tree.node([build(. - 1), build(. - 1)])\n\nfn count(t: Tree) -> Int =\n    t | Leaf -> 1 or Node -> 1 + count(.[0]!) + count(.[1]!)\n\ncount(build(parse(stdin)))","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-benchmark-fasta-build/benches/programs/binary-trees.toy","text":"enum Tree { Leaf, Node(Vec<Tree>) }\n\nfn build(depth: Int) -> Tree =\n    depth | . == 0 -> Tree.leaf or Tree.node([build(. - 1), build(. - 1)])\n\nfn count(t: Tree) -> Int =\n    t | Leaf -> 1 or Node -> 1 + count(.[0]!) + count(.[1]!)\n\ncount(build(parse(stdin)))","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":9,"totalLines":9,"truncated":false}},"title":"benches/programs/binary-trees.toy","time":{"start":1788686293000,"end":1788686293009}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The bench harness only feeds at most one Int value from stdin. The fasta task typically reads the length of the sequence from stdin (an integer). So fasta fits: it takes an Int input N and generates N lines of DNA sequence.\n\nLet me understand the CLBG fasta task. From the benchmarks game:\n\nThe fasta benchmark: generate DNA sequences. It takes an input N (length) and outputs:\n- One line of description\n- A random sequence of length N (using a particular algorithm with constants)\n- A repeated sequence (tandem repeats of \"GGCCGGGCGCGGTGGCTCACGCCTGTAATCCCAGCACTTTGGGAGGCCGAGGCGGGCGGATCACCTGAGGTCAGGAGTTCGAGACCAGCCTGGCCAACATGGTGAAACCCCGTCTCTACTAAAAATACAAAAATTAGCCGGGCGTGGTGGCGCGCGCCTGTAATCCCAGCTACTCGGGAGGCTGAGGCAGGAGAATCGCTTGAACCCGGGAGGCGGAGGTTGCAGTGAGCCGAGATCGCGCCACTGCACTCCAGCCTGGGCGACAGAGCGAGACTCCGTCTCAAAAA\")\n- A random sequence generated from another alphabet with weights (IUB codes), etc.\n\nThe CLBG fasta task (from the actual benchmark) has specific structure. Let me recall the actual spec. The task description:\n\n\"Generate and write random DNA sequences\"\n\nInput: a number N.\n\nOutput:\n```\n>ONE Homo sapiens alu\nGGCCGGGCGCGGTGGCTCACGCCTGTAATCCCAGCACTTTGGGAGGCCGAGGCGGGCGGATCACCTGAGGTCAGGAGTTCGAGACCAGCCTGGCCAACATGGTGAAACCCCGTCTCTACTAAAAATACAAAAATTAGCCGGGCGTGGTGGCGCGCGCCTGTAATCCCAGCTACTCGGGAGGCTGAGGCAGGAGAATCGCTTGAACCCGGGAGGCGGAGGTTGCAGTGAGCCGAGATCGCGCCACTGCACTCCAGCCTGGGCGACAGAGCGAGACTCCGTCTCAAAAA\n>THREE Homo sapiens frequency\n[random sequence of length N using weights]\n>FIVE Homo sapiens frequency\n[tandem repeats...]\n>TEN Homo sapiens frequency\n...\n```\n\nActually the CLBG fasta task has these sections:\n- `>ONE Homo sapiens alu` followed by the ALU repeat (a fixed string, repeated to length 2*N)\n- `>TWO IUB ambiguity codes` random sequence of length 3*N using IUB weighted alphabet\n- `>THREE Homo sapiens frequency` random sequence of length 5*N using frequency-weighted alphabet\n\nThe task description (from benchmarksgame):\n\n\"Generate and write random DNA sequences, as per the fasta benchmark. The program takes a single integer N on the command line. It prints three sequences: ...\"\n\nActually, let me be careful. The actual CLBG fasta spec:\n\nThe program generates a pseudo-random DNA sequence. It takes N as input.\n\nLooking at the actual reference description from the CLBG site:\n\n\"fasta: Generate random DNA sequences. Input: N = the number of bases to generate. Output is a FASTA file with:\n- >ONE Homo sapiens alu (the alu repeat, 2*N lines of 60 chars)\n- >TWO IUB ambiguity codes (3*N chars random)\n- >THREE Homo sapiens frequency (5*N chars random)\"\n\nHmm, but this is complex. The benchmark-plan says \"Write the programs the way this repo already treats Project Euler: paraphrase the problem, never copy a reference implementation.\"\n\nBut actually, the important thing is that the corpus case pins correctness (small N), and the bench input is larger. Since I'm paraphrasing from the plain description, I should implement something reasonable that matches the CLBG fasta task's spirit: generate random DNA sequences from a small literal Str alphabet via a computed PRNG index.\n\nThe task in my brief: \"It generates DNA sequences from a small literal Str alphabet via a computed PRNG index, so it needs no Char -> Str conversion.\"\n\nSo the key technical piece is the PRNG (the CLBG fasta uses a specific LCG). The fasta task uses the linear congruential generator:\n```\nIM = 139968\nIA = 3877\nIC = 29573\n```\nwith `last = (last * IA + IC) % IM`, and a random float `(last * 1.0 / IM)`.\n\nBut toylang is an Int language with Int arithmetic. The plan mentions fasta is \"fully numeric or literal-driven, no decode needed\". \n\nHmm. Let me think about what to implement. The brief says: \"implement the CLBG fasta benchmark task in toylang. It generates DNA sequences from a small literal Str alphabet via a computed PRNG index, so it needs no Char -> Str conversion.\"\n\nI need to decide the exact scope. Let me look at how the reference fasta actually works, since I need the correctness fixture to match something meaningful. Since I paraphrase, I define the exact output. The corpus case pins MY implementation's output at small N. So correctness is internal consistency: the small-N corpus case and the larger bench input both run the same program.\n\nLet me look at the actual CLBG fasta task to ground the design. Let me fetch the task description.\n\nActually, let me first understand toylang's capabilities better - what builtins exist, how to build strings, print, etc. Let me look at the existing programs and corpus to learn the idioms.\n\nLet me look at the fasta reference from CLBG. But since I need to paraphrase and not copy, let me think about the essential structure.\n\nThe classic CLBG fasta (Go version) logic:\n1. Read N.\n2. Define IM=139968, IA=3877, IC=29573, and a `last` seed = 42.\n3. `random()`: last = (last*IA+IC)%IM; return last/IM (a float in (0,1)).\n4. `makeCumulative` on a table of (char, weight).\n5. Print sections.\n\nBut toylang has no floats (mostly). Wait, the plan says n-body/spectral-norm need float support that most backends don't have yet. So toylang is integer-focused. So I should use integer arithmetic for the PRNG, which the plan explicitly says: \"a computed PRNG index\". So I generate an integer PRNG index and select from the alphabet. \n\nSo the design: a simple LCG producing an integer index into a small Str alphabet. E.g., for DNA, alphabet of 4 bases \"A\",\"C\",\"G\",\"T\". For weighted IUB, a larger alphabet.\n\nLet me keep it manageable and faithful to the CLBG task structure but integer-based:\n\nActually, given the plan says \"fasta is different: it only generates text from a small fixed alphabet of literal Str values chosen by a computed index\". And it says fasta and fannkuch-redux are \"both fully numeric or literal-driven, no decode needed\".\n\nLet me think about the concrete task. I'll implement the CLBG fasta task. Let me check the actual benchmark task description to know the output format precisely, since the corpus pins it. Let me fetch the CLBG fasta description.\n\nLet me fetch the benchmarks game fasta page.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07602d692001XnbPx2X8Y5UQgg","sessionID":"ses_f89fe2701ffe6lTPBHqSpApZWe","messageID":"msg_0760247af001rWBE0yg5TAcurc"}}
